WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.350
David Gordon Green's follow-up to John Carpenter's
classic Halloween is a very different kind

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.669
of sequel than most of its predecessors.

00:00:07.669 --> 00:00:12.500
That's because Carpenter himself has returned
to the series for the first time in 36 years

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.990
to serve as executive producer and composer.

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:19.089
Anticipation got even hotter when Jamie Lee
Curtis and Nick Castle were both revealed

00:00:19.089 --> 00:00:23.270
to be reprising their respective roles as
Laurie Strode and Michael Myers.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.359
Add in the facts that Judy Greer stars as
Laurie's daughter, and Danny McBride is a

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.630
co-writer on the film, and the Halloween hype
is real.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.590
The film's trailer debuted at CinemaCon in
April, but the footage remained otherwise

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.070
under wraps until now.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:39.220
Luckily, fans seem to agree it was worth the
wait.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.500
After all the guessing, which hints does the
trailer give us about what awaits in this

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.560
latest installment of the Halloween saga?

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.870
Join us as we cut into some of the details
you might have missed.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.610
Cleaning up the timeline

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:55.700
McBride hinted early on that the new film
wouldn't be particularly concerned with any

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:00.329
of the original film's other sequels, which
is understandable, given how muddied they've

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:02.200
become over the decades.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.580
After all, there've been multiple sequels
and remakes showing Laurie in various states

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:11.180
of duress - remember when she only had a daughter,
then somehow had a son?

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.240
Then there was that time she was dead, until
she wasn't…

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.090
it's become quite a mess at this point.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:20.470
But this movie lays out a new timeline with
a number of details that strip the story down

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.970
to its bones.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:26.560
Michael is cited as having murdered three
teenagers on Halloween night in 1978, which

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.600
is probably referring to poor Annie, Bob,
and Lynda.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.670
And he's been in custody ever since, meaning
definitively that no other sequels have happened

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.920
in the world of this new Halloween.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.159
Based on that body count, it seems even the
Carpenter-scripted Halloween II, which took

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:45.780
place on the same night as its predecessor,
wasn't safe from the purge.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:51.710
But hey, at least fans will only have to refresh
on the first film to get ready for this one.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:52.869
Sequel nods

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:57.210
The sequels may now be banished to an alternate
timeline, but that doesn't mean there aren't

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.280
echoes of them in the new Halloween.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.840
For instance, we're given a glimpse of a truck
stop setting not unlike one in which Dr. Sam

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.439
Loomis faced off with Michael in Halloween
4.

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:10.560
And a scene of the movie maniac stalking in
a public restroom, which will likely seem

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.870
familiar to anyone who remembers Halloween:
H20.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:18.510
The most significant reference to the sequels,
though, is definitely a line in which Laurie's

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:23.370
granddaughter, Allyson, clarifies that Michael
Myers is not, in fact, her grandmother's brother.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.370
"No, it was not her brother.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.880
That was something that people made up."

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.980
That shuts down one of the core tenets of
the Halloween mythos.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.849
The sibling connection was first revealed
in Halloween II and would be explored and

00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:38.120
explained throughout the series as an ancient
curse that drove Michael to slaughter his

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.350
own bloodline.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:44.459
With this line of dialogue, the trailer acknowledges
this legacy while simultaneously wiping the

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:51.000
slate clean and returning to the elemental
horror of Michael Myers' unknowable motivations.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.780
Loomis looms large

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.230
Donald Pleasence's performance as Dr. Sam
Loomis has always one of the most consistent

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:02.150
elements of the Halloween series, as he dedicated
his life to stopping Michael Myers by any

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:03.290
means necessary.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:09.560
Sadly, Pleasence passed away just after completing
work on the franchise's sixth movie, 1995's

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.040
Halloween: The Curse of Michael Myers.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.900
But no tribute to the original classic would
be complete without an acknowledgement of

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.090
the good doctor.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:22.209
As the trailer begins, we meet two documentarians
preparing to interview Michael in prison.

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:26.989
Quick flashes show us excerpts from Loomis'
notes, including first-hand accounts of his

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:31.540
early encounters with the young boy who had
just slayed his sister Judith.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:36.430
We also see a courtroom sketch of Loomis testifying
at Michael's trial, hinting at the events

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.440
that immediately followed Halloween night,
1978 in this new timeline.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.849
Hopefully, we'll get to find out how the rest
of the doctor's life transpired in a world

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:48.489
where he didn't have to fight his greatest
nemesis over and over again.

00:03:48.489 --> 00:03:53.019
Appropriately, this montage is closed out
by the very same sound of Loomis' gunshots

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:57.440
that sent Michael over a balcony at the end
of the original movie.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.500
Eerie echoes

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.770
While the trailer promises that director David
Gordon Green is bringing a unique and striking

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:08.560
visual style to Halloween, it also grants
a few nods to specific moments from the original

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:09.560
movie.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.120
A shot of escaped inmates wandering on a road
in a car's headlights immediately recalls

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.960
Dr. Loomis' very first scene.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:21.079
A shape waits menacingly under a cute ghost
sheet, just as Michael did before killing

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:23.160
Lynda in 1978.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.440
Some might even say that Will Patton, in his
role as a Haddonfield police officer, bears

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.130
a striking resemblance to Charles Cyphers
as Sheriff Brackett.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:37.000
For Green and McBride, recapturing Carpenter's
aesthetic in a fresh light was a major priority.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.110
As McBride told Collider:

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.440
"It seemed to be such a simple formula of
what made it so spooky and I haven't even

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.630
seen that duplicated in the other ones."

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:48.970
That spirit is certainly alive in the trailer's
shot of Michael Myers taunting a would-be

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.860
victim with a handful of teeth.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.330
Laurie's life

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:57.630
Halloween made both Jamie Lee Curtis and the
character of Laurie Strode iconic.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:01.660
The series has long struggled with the task
of living up to her original performance.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:06.680
She remained bedridden for most of the first
sequel, then supposedly died in a car accident,

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:10.520
only to have the character return in H20 under
an assumed identity.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:15.040
Finally, she was taken out by Michael in the
opening minutes of Halloween: Resurrection.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.310
So, if this new Halloween ignores all but
that one night of terror, what's Laurie been

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:21.900
up to for 40 years?

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.350
Preparing, it seems.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:25.270
"What the hell did you do that for?"

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:27.530
"So I can kill him."

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:32.330
Our first glimpse of Laurie in the trailer
is of the scar she still bears from Michael's

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:33.330
knife.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:36.620
It's a clear visual metaphor for her refusal
to forget about him.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:41.500
We see her practicing her marksmanship and
declaring her intention to wait for his return,

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.130
and she seems to have a secret bunker of some
kind.

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:47.270
The spree begins

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:52.720
The trailer's brief recap of the events of
1978 serves as a reminder that the body count

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.270
of the original Halloween is surprisingly
low - at least by the standards of the slasher

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.590
films that would follow it.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:03.710
Rather than gore and relentless brutality,
it relied on unsettling tension and creative

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:04.710
kills.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:09.080
It remains to be seen just how relentless
Michael Myers' return will be, but the trailer

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.949
does offer some quick glances at the murderous
mayhem to come.

00:06:12.949 --> 00:06:17.370
Michael's pursuit of the documentarian, Dana,
as she hides in a bathroom stall is far from

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:18.370
subtle.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:22.551
But blink and you might miss that one of his
tools in this scene is the bloodied body of

00:06:22.551 --> 00:06:27.669
Dana's partner, Martin, who is being used
by the maniac as a human battering ram to

00:06:27.669 --> 00:06:29.050
break down the door.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:33.510
We also spot an unfortunate mechanic who falls
victim to Michael's need for a new pair of

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:34.650
overalls.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:39.180
Another man can be seen getting attacked from
behind as a horrified Allyson watches.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.759
Finally, a shot of a yellow-sleeved arm grasping
desperately for help suggests that Myers hasn't

00:06:44.759 --> 00:06:47.770
lost his penchant for offing innocent babysitters.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:52.039
Of course the final victim tally will have
to wait until October 19th - the Night He

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:53.930
Comes Home… again.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:54.930
Thanks for watching!

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.710
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:59.710
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

